Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Structural signal discovery.} We demonstrate that 30 lightweight structural features achieve near-perfect stagnation detection (0.995 in-domain, 0.967 cross-agent macro-F1) with zero target data, outperforming the probe zero-shot by 36.9\,pp and the LLM-as-judge by 30.4\,pp, with no GPU and under 5\,ms per window. With full fine-tuning the probe reaches within 1\,pp of the structural baseline (0.976 vs.\ 0.967 macro-F1), delineating when structural monitoring suffices and when fine-tuning is warranted.
    \item \textbf{Label quality as transfer bottleneck.} We show that labeling functions exhibit agent-specific temporal reversals and that fixing label version bias (stag rate 6.56\%$\to$21.4\%) dominates LF selection for cross-agent transfer, informing future weak-supervision design on sequential agent data.
    \item \textbf{Label model negative finding.} We show that five label models---three temporal and two generative---fail to improve over majority vote for downstream detection, despite clear temporal structure in LF firing patterns. The bottleneck is LF quantity, not aggregation method: the 3-LF regime produces insufficient inter-LF conflict for any approach to add value.
\end{enumerate}

